(window.LADDER_CARDS["27a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27a1.1","q":"What is a space of vectors, and what does the zero-stretch technique say?","a":"a set whose members add and stretch by numbers under the eight conditions; in every such space 0·v = 0, t·0 = 0 and (−1)·v = −v","w":["a set of arrows in the plane or in space only; 0·v = v and (−1)·v = −v","a set whose members add and multiply each other; 0·v = 0 and t·v = 0 only when t = 0","any set that holds a 0; t·0 = t and (−1)·v = v"],"why":"Lists, polynomials and solutions of y″ = −y are all spaces of vectors."},
 {"u":"27a2.1","q":"What does the inside-space technique say, and why need the eight conditions not be checked again in W?","a":"a part W of a space of vectors that holds 0 and is kept by adding and by stretching is itself a space of vectors; the conditions already hold for every vector of the larger space, so they hold for those in W","w":["any part of a space of vectors is a space of vectors; the conditions are inherited","a part W that holds 0 is a space of vectors; adding and stretching need not stay in W","a part W kept by adding is a space of vectors; the eight conditions must each be proved again in W"],"why":"Three checks stand in for eight."},
 {"u":"27a3.1","q":"What is wrong with “{(x, y) : x + y = 1} is a space of vectors”?","a":"it does not hold 0, and adding leaves it: (1, 0) + (0, 1) = (1, 1), whose entries add to 2","w":["it holds 0 but stretching leaves it","it is a line, and no line is a space of vectors","nothing; a line is always a space of vectors"],"why":"The line x + y = 0 through 0 is one; this one misses 0."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27b1.1","q":"What does it mean for vectors to build V, and to be free, and what does the spare-vector technique say?","a":"they build V when every vector of V is a mix of them, and are free when only the mix with every number 0 gives 0; if a list with v₁ ≠ 0 is not free, some vⱼ is a mix of those before it, and dropping it loses no mix","w":["they build V when they lie in V, and are free when no two are equal; a list that is not free holds the zero vector","they build V when they are free, and are free when they build V; dropping any vector of a list loses no mix","they build V when some mix of them is 0, and are free when every mix is different from 0; the last vector of a list is always spare"],"why":"A spare vector adds nothing the others cannot make."},
 {"u":"27b2.1","q":"What does the exchange technique say, and why is the spare that goes out never one of the free vectors?","a":"if m vectors build V and k vectors of V are free, then k ≤ m; each free u is put in at the front, and the spare is a mix of the vectors before it, which cannot be a u among u’s because the u’s are free","w":["if m vectors build V and k are free, then k ≥ m; the spare is always the newest u","if m vectors build V then exactly m are free; the spare is chosen at will","if k free vectors lie in V then they build V; no spare is needed"],"why":"No free list is longer than a building list."},
 {"u":"27b3.1","q":"What is the dimension of a space, why is it well defined, and what does the grow-to-basis technique say?","a":"the number of vectors in a basis, a free list that builds the space; any two bases have the same number, by the exchange technique used both ways; and in dimension n every free list grows into a basis, and n free vectors already are one","w":["the largest entry in its lists; it is well defined by choice; any n vectors are a basis","the number of vectors in the space; it is well defined because the space is a set; every list can be shrunk to a basis of any size","the number of vectors in a basis; it depends on the basis chosen; n vectors that build the space need not be free"],"why":"Polynomials of degree at most n have dimension n + 1."},
 {"u":"27b4.1","q":"What is wrong with “each pair of the list is free, so the list is free”?","a":"freedom is a demand on the whole list: every pair of (1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1) is free, and yet the third is the sum of the first two","w":["nothing; a list is free exactly when its pairs are","the pairs must be checked in order, first with second, second with third","it fails only for lists that hold the zero vector"],"why":"Three vectors in a space of dimension 2 are never free."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27c1.1","q":"What is a straight map, what does it lose and reach, and what does the nothing-sent technique say?","a":"a map that keeps adding and stretching; it loses the vectors it sends to 0 and reaches the vectors L(v); both are spaces, and L is one-to-one exactly when it loses only 0","w":["a map whose graph is a line; it loses its inputs and reaches its outputs; it is one-to-one exactly when it reaches everything","a map that keeps adding; what it loses is a space only when L is one-to-one","a map that keeps lengths; it loses nothing and reaches everything"],"why":"L(u) = L(v) exactly when L(u − v) = 0."},
 {"u":"27c2.1","q":"What does the lost-plus-reached technique say, and what does it give for a straight map from a space of dimension n to itself?","a":"the dimension of what L loses plus the dimension of what it reaches is n, the dimension of the space it starts from; so from a space to itself, L is one-to-one exactly when it reaches everything","w":["lost plus reached is the dimension of the space L lands in; so every map to itself is one-to-one","lost times reached is n; so a map to itself loses nothing","lost plus reached is n; so a map from a space to itself always reaches everything"],"why":"What is not lost is reached."},
 {"u":"27c3.1","q":"What is wrong with “L(x) = 2x + 1 is straight”?","a":"a straight map sends 0 to 0 and keeps adding; this one sends 0 to 1, and L(x + y) = 2x + 2y + 1 is not L(x) + L(y)","w":["nothing; its graph is a straight line","it fails only because 2 ≠ 1","it keeps adding but not stretching"],"why":"Straight here means keeping mixes, not having a line for a graph."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27d1.1","q":"What is a grid, how does it act on a list, and what does the columns technique say?","a":"an array of numbers with m rows and n columns; Ax is the mix of its columns with the numbers of x, so (Ax)ᵢ = aᵢ₁x₁ + ⋯ + aᵢₙxₙ; and every straight map from lists of n to lists of m is x ↦ Ax for exactly one grid, whose column j is L(eⱼ)","w":["an array of numbers; Ax multiplies place by place; a straight map has many grids","an array whose row i is L(eᵢ); Ax is the mix of the rows of A","a square array only; every map, straight or not, is x ↦ Ax for one grid"],"why":"A straight map is known once it is known on e₁, …, eₙ."},
 {"u":"27d2.1","q":"What does the product-is-doing technique say, and why is the product grouping-free but not turn-about?","a":"doing B and then A is the grid AB, whose column j is A times column j of B; doing maps in a row is grouping-free, so (AB)C = A(BC), but the order of doing matters: a quarter turn after a flip is not the flip after the quarter turn","w":["AB is doing A and then B; it is turn-about because numbers multiply turn-about","AB multiplies entries place by place; it is both grouping-free and turn-about","AB is doing B and then A; it is turn-about but not grouping-free"],"why":"Multiplying grids is running maps in a row."},
 {"u":"27d3.1","q":"What is an undoable grid, and what does the undoable-group technique say?","a":"a square grid A with a grid A⁻¹ such that A⁻¹A = AA⁻¹ = I; the undoable n × n grids form a group under the product, and a square grid is undoable as soon as it loses only 0","w":["any grid with no zero entry; the undoable grids form a group under adding","a square grid with A² = I; a square grid is undoable as soon as it has no zero column","a grid with more columns than rows; it is undoable when it reaches everything"],"why":"Losing only 0 gives reaching everything, by lost plus reached."},
 {"u":"27d4.1","q":"What is wrong with “(AB)⁻¹ = A⁻¹B⁻¹”?","a":"undo the last first: AB does B and then A, so its undo is A⁻¹ first and then B⁻¹, which is the grid B⁻¹A⁻¹","w":["it should read (AB)⁻¹ = A⁻¹ + B⁻¹","nothing; grids multiply turn-about","a product of undoable grids cannot be undone"],"why":"The order only stops mattering when A and B commute."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27e1.1","q":"What are the three conditions straight, flat and unit, and what does the column-slide technique say?","a":"D is straight in each column with the others held, D is 0 when two columns are equal, and D(I) = 1; from straight and flat, swapping two columns changes the sign, adding a stretch of one column to another changes nothing, and a zero column gives 0","w":["D keeps adding of grids, D is 0 for the zero grid, and D(I) = 1; swapping two columns changes nothing","D is straight in each column, D is 1 when two columns are equal, and D(I) = 0; a zero column gives 1","D is straight in each row only, flat means D ≥ 0, and unit means |D| ≤ 1; adding one column to another doubles D"],"why":"Three demands fix one number for each grid."},
 {"u":"27e2.1","q":"What does the three-conditions technique say, and what is the determinant of a 2 × 2 grid?","a":"exactly one D obeys all three: the sum over the shuffles σ of sign(σ) a_{σ(1)1} ⋯ a_{σ(n)n}, and a D that is only straight and flat is D(I) times it; for a 2 × 2 grid it is ad − bc, the signed area of the parallelogram on the columns","w":["many D obey all three; for a 2 × 2 grid one of them is ad + bc","exactly one D obeys all three: the product of the entries down the middle; for a 2 × 2 grid it is ad","exactly one D obeys all three: the sum of all the entries; for a 2 × 2 grid it is a + b + c + d"],"why":"One term for each shuffle, signed even or odd."},
 {"u":"27e3.1","q":"What does the det-product technique say, and how does its proof use the three-conditions technique?","a":"det(AB) = det A · det B; hold A and let D(B) = det(AB): it is straight and flat in the columns of B, so D(B) = D(I) det B, and D(I) = det A","w":["det(AB) = det A + det B; the proof adds the two sums over shuffles","det(AB) = det A · det B; the proof multiplies out both sums over shuffles term by term","det(AB) = det(BA) only; the proof swaps the columns of A and B"],"why":"Uniqueness does the work that multiplying out would."},
 {"u":"27e4.1","q":"What does the nonzero-det technique say, and why does a grid that cannot be undone have determinant 0?","a":"a square grid can be undone exactly when its determinant is not 0; one that cannot loses more than 0, so a column is a mix of the others, and sliding that mix off leaves a zero column with the same det","w":["a square grid can be undone exactly when its determinant is 1; otherwise a column is 0","a square grid can be undone exactly when its determinant is above 0; a negative det means a swapped column","a grid can be undone when no entry is 0; a zero entry gives det 0"],"why":"The other way: det A · det A⁻¹ = det I = 1."},
 {"u":"27e5.1","q":"What is wrong with “det(A + B) = det A + det B”?","a":"the determinant is straight in one column at a time, not in the whole grid: det I + det I = 2, while det(2I) = 2ⁿ","w":["it holds for 2 × 2 grids and fails above","it should read det(A + B) = det A · det B","nothing; det keeps adding as it keeps products"],"why":"Each of the n columns is stretched: det(tA) = tⁿ det A."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27f1.1","q":"What is a vector only stretched by A, and its stretch, and what does the stretch-test technique say?","a":"a v ≠ 0 with Av = λv, and λ is its stretch; λ is a stretch of A exactly when det(A − λI) = 0, a polynomial in λ of degree n, so every square grid has a stretch once complex numbers are allowed","w":["any v with Av = λv, the zero vector included; λ is a stretch when det A = λ","a v ≠ 0 with Av = v; the stretch is always 1","a v ≠ 0 with Av = λv; λ is a stretch exactly when det(A − λI) ≠ 0, so every real grid has a real stretch"],"why":"A − λI must lose more than 0."},
 {"u":"27f2.1","q":"What does the different-stretches technique say, and where does its proof use that the stretches differ?","a":"vectors only stretched by A, with stretches all different, are free; if vⱼ were a mix of those before, applying A and subtracting λⱼ times the mix leaves each cᵢ(λᵢ − λⱼ) = 0, and since λᵢ ≠ λⱼ every cᵢ is 0, so vⱼ = 0","w":["stretched vectors are always free, whatever their stretches; the stretches are never used","stretched vectors with different stretches are at right angles; the proof takes their dot","stretched vectors with equal stretches are free; the proof divides by λᵢ − λⱼ = 0"],"why":"So n different stretches give a basis of stretched vectors."},
 {"u":"27f3.1","q":"What does the straighten-out technique say, and why can the slide with rows (1, 1), (0, 1) not be straightened out?","a":"n free stretched vectors, set as the columns of P, give A = P D P⁻¹ with the stretches down the middle of D, and Aᵏ = P Dᵏ P⁻¹; the slide’s only stretch is 1, and every vector it only stretches lies on the floor, so there are not two free ones","w":["every grid is P D P⁻¹; the slide is straightened with D = I","A = P D P⁻¹ whenever det A ≠ 0; the slide fails because its det is 0","A = D P whenever its stretches are real; the slide fails because its stretch is repeated, as I’s is"],"why":"I repeats its stretch too, and is already straight: what fails is the count of vectors."},
 {"u":"27f4.1","q":"What is wrong with “Av = λv with v = 0 makes λ a stretch”?","a":"A·0 = λ·0 for every λ, so it singles out nothing; a stretch needs a vector other than 0 that is only stretched","w":["v = 0 makes only λ = 0 a stretch","nothing; the zero vector is stretched by every grid","v = 0 is allowed when A cannot be undone"],"why":"Otherwise every number would be a stretch of every grid."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27g1.1","q":"What is the transpose of A, what does the side-turn technique say, and what does it hand to rows?","a":"Aᵀ is A turned on its side, rows written as columns; det Aᵀ = det A; so every column technique holds for rows: a row swap changes the sign, and adding a stretch of one row to another changes nothing","w":["Aᵀ is A with its columns reversed; det Aᵀ = −det A; rows obey the opposite techniques","Aᵀ is the undo of A; det Aᵀ = 1/det A; rows obey no techniques","Aᵀ is A turned on its side; det Aᵀ = det A; but a row swap leaves det alone"],"why":"The sum over shuffles runs through σ⁻¹ as well as σ."},
 {"u":"27g2.1","q":"What is the stand-in Cᵢⱼ, how is the stand-in grid S built, and what does the stand-in technique say?","a":"Cᵢⱼ is (−1)^(i + j) times the det of A with row i and column j struck; S has Cᵢⱼ in place j, i, the stand-ins turned on their side; S A = (det A) I, so when det A ≠ 0, A⁻¹ = S/det A","w":["Cᵢⱼ is the det of A with row i and column j struck, with no sign; S has Cᵢⱼ in place i, j; S A = I","Cᵢⱼ is aᵢⱼ with its sign changed; S = −A; S A = −(det A) I","Cᵢⱼ is (−1)^(i + j) aᵢⱼ; S is its transpose; S A = det A"],"why":"Off the middle, a column appears twice, and flat gives 0."},
 {"u":"27g3.1","q":"What does the own-polynomial technique say, and why is putting λ = A into det(A − λI) not allowed?","a":"with p(λ) = det(A − λI), the grid p(A) = c₀I + c₁A + ⋯ + cₙAⁿ is the zero grid; p(A) is a grid, while det(A − λI) with a grid in place of the number λ is not sensibly written, and its det would be a number","w":["p(A) = I; putting λ = A is the proof","p(A) = 0 because det(A − A) = det 0 = 0, which is allowed for undoable A only","p(A) = det A · I; putting λ = A is not allowed because A may have no real stretch"],"why":"The stand-in grids make the substitution honest."},
 {"u":"27g4.1","q":"What does the least-obeyed technique say?","a":"among the polynomials with first coefficient 1 that A obeys there is one of least degree, m; every polynomial A obeys is a multiple of m, and every stretch of A is a root of m","w":["the least polynomial A obeys is always its stretch polynomial","every polynomial A obeys has degree exactly n; its roots need not be stretches","the least polynomial A obeys is λ − det A; every stretch divides it"],"why":"Divide with remainder: a nonzero remainder would be obeyed with smaller degree."},
 {"u":"27g5.1","q":"What is wrong with “p(A) = 0, so p is the smallest polynomial A obeys”?","a":"the own polynomial is obeyed but need not be least: I obeys λ − 1, of degree 1, while its stretch polynomial is (1 − λ)ⁿ","w":["p(A) is never 0, so the premise fails","the smallest is always of degree n + 1","nothing; the stretch polynomial is always the least"],"why":"The least one divides it."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27h1.1","q":"What is a reading of a space V, and what does the reading-basis technique say?","a":"a straight map from V to the numbers; for a basis v₁, …, vₙ, the readings fᵢ with fᵢ(vⱼ) = 1 when i = j and 0 otherwise are a basis of the space V* of readings, so V* has dimension n","w":["a vector of V of length 1; the readings are the basis itself","any map from V to the numbers; V* has endlessly many dimensions","a straight map from V to V; V* has dimension n²"],"why":"V matches V* only by a choice of basis, and V** with none."},
 {"u":"27h2.1","q":"What does the two-slot technique say, and when is B(x, y) = B(y, x) for all x and y?","a":"a B that is straight in each slot with the other held is B(x, y) = xᵀGy, with Gᵢⱼ = B(eᵢ, eⱼ); and it is the same both ways round exactly when G = Gᵀ","w":["B(x, y) = Gx + Gy; it is the same both ways round for every G","B(x, y) = xᵀGy with G = I always; so it is always the same both ways round","B(x, y) = xᵀGy; it is the same both ways round exactly when det G = 1"],"why":"With G = I it is the dot, and B(x, x) the square of the length."},
 {"u":"27h3.1","q":"Why is f(x) = x₁ + 1 not a reading, and why is B(x, y) = x₁y₁ not straight in the pair (x, y)?","a":"f(0) = 1, and a straight map sends 0 to 0; and stretching the pair by t stretches B by t², since it is straight in each slot separately, not in both at once","w":["f is not a reading because it uses only x₁; B is straight in the pair","f is a reading; B fails because it leaves out x₂ and y₂","f fails because 1 is not a vector; B fails because it is not the same both ways round"],"why":"Two-slot straight is not straight."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27i1.1","q":"What are the dot and the length of lists of n numbers, and what does the angle-between technique say?","a":"u · v = u₁v₁ + ⋯ + uₙvₙ and |u| = √(u · u); u · v/(|u| |v|) lies between −1 and 1, so exactly one θ between 0 and π has that cosine, and with it |u − v|² = |u|² + |v|² − 2|u| |v| cos θ","w":["u · v is the list of products and |u| the sum of entries; the angle exists only in the plane and in space","u · v = u₁v₁ + ⋯ + uₙvₙ and |u| = u · u; the ratio can be any number, so θ is chosen by picture","u · v = |u| + |v| and |u| the largest entry; exactly two angles have that cosine"],"why":"In forty dimensions there is nothing to draw, and the angle is still one number."},
 {"u":"27i2.1","q":"What does the read-off technique say, and why are measuring directions chosen at right angles whenever they can be?","a":"n vectors of length 1, each at right angles to the others, are a basis, and every u is (u · d₁)d₁ + ⋯ + (u · dₙ)dₙ; each number is read off by one dot, with no system of samenesses to solve","w":["any n vectors of length 1 are a basis, and u is the sum of its dots; right angles only make the picture neater","n vectors at right angles are free but may not build the space; the numbers are found by elimination","every u is (u · d₁) + ⋯ + (u · dₙ); right angles are chosen so the dots are 0"],"why":"Each direction answers for itself and for no other."},
 {"u":"27i3.1","q":"What is the cross u × v, and what does the cross technique say about it?","a":"(u₂v₃ − u₃v₂, u₃v₁ − u₁v₃, u₁v₂ − u₂v₁); it is at right angles to u and to v, v × u = −(u × v), its length is |u| |v| sin θ, the area of the parallelogram, and it is 0 exactly when u and v are not free","w":["(u₁v₁, u₂v₂, u₃v₃); it lies in the plane of u and v, and u × v = v × u","the number u₁v₁ + u₂v₂ + u₃v₃; it is 0 exactly when u and v are at right angles","(u₂v₃ − u₃v₂, u₃v₁ − u₁v₃, u₁v₂ − u₂v₁); its length is |u| |v| cos θ, and it is 0 when u and v are at right angles"],"why":"It exists only in space."},
 {"u":"27i4.1","q":"What does the moving-dot technique say, and why does a vector of fixed length move at right angles to itself?","a":"(u · v)′ = u′ · v + u · v′ and (u × v)′ = u′ × v + u × v′; if r · r is the same for every t, its slope 2 r · r′ is 0, so r′ is at right angles to r","w":["(u · v)′ = u′ · v′; a vector of fixed length does not move","(u · v)′ = u′ · v − u · v′; a vector of fixed length moves along itself","(u · v)′ = u′ · v + u · v′; a vector of fixed length has r′ = 0"],"why":"A stone on a string: its velocity is always across the string."},
 {"u":"27i5.1","q":"What is wrong with “(u × v) × w = u × (v × w)”, and with “u · v = 0, so u = 0 or v = 0”?","a":"the cross is not grouping-free: (e₁ × e₁) × e₂ = 0 while e₁ × (e₁ × e₂) = −e₂; and a dot of 0 means at right angles, as (1, 0) · (0, 1) = 0","w":["the cross is grouping-free but not turn-about; and the second is right, as for numbers","both hold in space and fail only in the plane","the first fails only when u = v; the second fails only for vectors of length 1"],"why":"Neither product behaves like the product of numbers."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27j1.1","q":"What are the three row moves, and why does a row move on [A | b] keep exactly the same answers?","a":"swap two rows, stretch a row by a number that is not 0, and add a stretch of one row to another; each is multiplying on the left by an undoable grid E, so Ax = b gives EAx = Eb, and the undo, itself a row move, gives it back","w":["swap two rows, stretch a row by any number, and add one column to another; each keeps det, so the answers stay","swap two columns, stretch a column, and add a stretch of one column to another; each renames the unknowns","delete a row, copy a row, and add two rows; each loses no sameness"],"why":"Undoable both ways: no answer is gained and none is lost."},
 {"u":"27j2.1","q":"When is a grid in lowest terms, and what does the grid-lowest-terms technique say?","a":"when each row’s lead is 1, each lead stands to the right of the lead above with zeros in the rest of its column, and the zero rows are last; row moves bring every grid to such a grid, and to only one","w":["when every entry is 0 or 1; row moves bring a grid to many such grids","when every entry below the middle is 0; that staircase is unique","when its rows are free; only undoable grids have lowest terms"],"why":"A column with no lead is a free choice in the answers."},
 {"u":"27j3.1","q":"What does the undo-by-rows technique say, and what does the elimination-cost technique say?","a":"a square grid can be undone exactly when row moves bring it to I, and the same moves done to I give A⁻¹; and n samenesses in n unknowns cost n³/3 + n² − n/3 multiplications and divisions, so about n³/3: doubling n multiplies the work by about 8","w":["a square grid is undone by turning it on its side; the cost is about n², so doubling n multiplies it by 4","the row moves done to A⁻¹ give I; the cost is n! multiplications","a square grid can be undone when row moves bring it to a staircase; the cost is about n, so doubling n doubles it"],"why":"At n = 1000 that is about 334 million, under a second."},
 {"u":"27j4.1","q":"What is wrong with stretching a row by 0, and with adding a stretch of one column to another?","a":"stretching by 0 cannot be undone and wipes out a sameness, so answers are gained; and a column move mixes the unknowns, so the answers of the new system are not those of the old","w":["stretching by 0 is allowed since 0 is a number; a column move is the same as a row move","stretching by 0 changes det only; a column move keeps the answers but changes their order","both are fine so long as b is moved too"],"why":"Row moves keep the answers; column moves keep only the det techniques."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27k"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27k"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27k1.1","q":"What does the stair-det technique say, and why is it so much faster than the formula over shuffles?","a":"with every entry below the middle 0, det is the product down the middle; so after row moves with s swaps and otherwise only adding stretches of rows, det A is (−1)ˢ times that product; the stairs cost about n³/3, the shuffles n! terms","w":["det is the sum down the middle; the stairs cost n! and the shuffles n³","det is the product down the middle, whatever row moves were used, stretches included; both cost the same","det is the product of all the entries of the staircase; the stairs are faster only for n < 10"],"why":"For n = 20: about three thousand against 2.4 × 10¹⁸."},
 {"u":"27k2.1","q":"What does the expansion technique say, and what is the stand-in Cᵢⱼ?","a":"along any column j, det A = a₁ⱼC₁ⱼ + ⋯ + aₙⱼCₙⱼ, and along any row likewise; Cᵢⱼ is (−1)^(i + j) times the det of A with row i and column j struck, the signs running like a chessboard","w":["det A = a₁ⱼ + ⋯ + aₙⱼ along any column; Cᵢⱼ is the entry aᵢⱼ itself","det A is the sum of aᵢⱼ times the det with row i and column j struck, with no signs","det A can be expanded along the first row only; Cᵢⱼ is (−1)^(ij) times the struck det"],"why":"The six-term diagonal pattern stops at three rows: four rows have 24 terms."},
 {"u":"27k3.1","q":"What does the det-ratio technique say, and which condition of the determinant makes every term but one vanish in its proof?","a":"if det A ≠ 0, Ax = b has exactly one answer, and xⱼ = det Aⱼ/det A, with Aⱼ being A with column j replaced by b; b is the mix of the columns with the numbers of x, and by flat every term with a repeated column is 0, leaving xⱼ det A","w":["xⱼ = det A/det Aⱼ; the unit technique removes the other terms","xⱼ = det Aⱼ − det A; the swap technique removes the other terms","xⱼ = bⱼ/det A; no terms vanish"],"why":"Its worth is the formula: for many unknowns one elimination is faster."},
 {"u":"27k4.1","q":"What is wrong with “stretching a row by c leaves det alone”, and with xⱼ = det Aⱼ/det A when det A = 0?","a":"det is straight in each row, so stretching a row by c multiplies det by c; and with det A = 0 there is no ratio to take: the system has no answer or many","w":["stretching a row changes only the sign; and with det A = 0 every xⱼ is 0","stretching a row by c multiplies det by cⁿ; and with det A = 0 the ratio is endless, which is the answer","both are right, since row moves keep the answers"],"why":"Only adding a stretch of one row to another leaves det alone."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27l"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27l"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27l1.1","q":"What is the middle sum of a square grid, and what does the middle-sum technique say?","a":"a₁₁ + ⋯ + aₙₙ, the sum down the middle; the n stretches of A in ℂ, counted with repeats, add to the middle sum and multiply to det A, and P⁻¹AP has the same stretch polynomial as A","w":["the sum of all the entries; the stretches add to det A and multiply to the middle sum","the product down the middle; the stretches are the entries down the middle","the sum down the middle; it equals the largest stretch, and changes under P⁻¹AP"],"why":"For a 2 × 2 grid the stretches are the roots of λ² − (a₁₁ + a₂₂)λ + det A."},
 {"u":"27l2.1","q":"What does the leading-stretch technique say?","a":"if A only stretches the free v₁, …, vₙ by λ₁, …, λₙ and x = c₁v₁ + ⋯ + cₙvₙ, then Aᵏx = c₁λ₁ᵏv₁ + ⋯ + cₙλₙᵏvₙ; when |λ₁| is the largest and c₁ ≠ 0, Aᵏx/λ₁ᵏ heads to c₁v₁","w":["Aᵏx heads to 0 for every x","Aᵏx/λ₁ᵏ heads to x, whatever the stretches","Aᵏx = k(c₁λ₁v₁ + ⋯ + cₙλₙvₙ), so every part grows alike"],"why":"For large k the largest stretch wins."},
 {"u":"27l3.1","q":"What does the step-grid technique say?","a":"if uₙ₊₁ = p uₙ + q uₙ₋₁, then (uₙ₊₁, uₙ) = Mⁿ(u₁, u₀) with M having rows (p, q), (1, 0); its stretches are the roots of λ² = pλ + q, and when they are two different numbers λ and μ, uₙ = cλⁿ + dμⁿ with c, d fixed by u₀ and u₁","w":["uₙ = n(p + q); the grid M has rows (p, 0), (0, q)","uₙ = cλⁿ with λ = p + q; one number c is fixed by u₀","uₙ = cλⁿ + dμⁿ where λ and μ are p and q themselves"],"why":"A two-step technique is one step of a grid."},
 {"u":"27l4.1","q":"What is wrong with “the stretches of A + B are sums of stretches of A and of B”, and with “row moves keep the stretches”?","a":"each grid stretches its own vectors, and a vector only stretched by A need not be one for B; and row moves keep the answers of Ax = b, not the stretches: rows (4, 1), (2, 3) have 5 and 2, and after one row move 4 and 5/2","w":["the first holds for all grids; the second fails only for swaps","the first fails only for grids that cannot be undone; row moves keep the stretches but not det","both hold, since det and the middle sum are kept"],"why":"P⁻¹AP keeps the stretches; EA does not."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27m"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27m"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27m1.1","q":"What does the length-keeper technique say, and what is a turn of space?","a":"keeping every length, keeping every dot, and QᵀQ = I are the same demand on a square grid Q; then Q⁻¹ = Qᵀ and det Q is 1 or −1; a turn is a length keeper with det 1, and one with det −1 is a mirror","w":["Q keeps lengths exactly when det Q = 1; a turn is any grid with det 1","Q keeps lengths exactly when Q = Qᵀ; a turn is a length keeper with det −1","Q keeps lengths when its columns have length 1; a turn is any length keeper"],"why":"The mirror of the floor keeps lengths and swaps the hands."},
 {"u":"27m2.1","q":"What does the axis technique say, and why does its proof that det(Q − I) = 0 need an odd number of directions?","a":"every turn Q of space keeps some u ≠ 0 in place and turns the plane at right angles to u by one θ, with middle sum 1 + 2 cos θ; the proof reaches det(Q − I) = (−1)³ det(Q − I), which forces 0 only because (−1)³ = −1; in the plane (−1)² = 1 says nothing","w":["every length keeper has an axis; the proof works in any number of directions","every turn of space keeps a plane in place; the proof needs an even number of directions","every turn of space has middle sum 3; the proof needs det Q = −1"],"why":"A quarter turn of the plane moves every vector but 0."},
 {"u":"27m3.1","q":"What does the axis-turn technique say, and what does its second statement settle that the middle sum cannot?","a":"the turn by θ about a unit u sends x to cos θ x + sin θ (u × x) + (1 − cos θ)(u · x)u; and sin θ has the sign of det[w Qw u], which settles which way round the turn goes, since the middle sum gives only cos θ","w":["the turn sends x to cos θ x + sin θ u; the second statement settles the size of θ","the turn sends x to x + θ (u × x); the second statement settles the axis","the turn sends x to cos θ x + sin θ (u × x); the second statement settles det Q"],"why":"θ and −θ have the same cosine."},
 {"u":"27m4.1","q":"What is wrong with “det Q = 1, so Q keeps lengths”, and with “−I is a turn of space, with an axis”?","a":"det 1 is needed for a turn but is not enough: the slide with rows (1, 1), (0, 1) has det 1 and stretches lengths; and −I keeps lengths but det(−I) = (−1)³ = −1, and it moves every vector but 0","w":["det Q = 1 is enough in the plane; −I is a half turn about every axis","det Q = 1 means Q = I; −I is a turn because (−I)² = I","both hold, since lengths and det go together"],"why":"A turn needs QᵀQ = I and det Q = 1, both."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27·cb24"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27·cb24"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27·cb24.1.1","q":"What does the split-system technique say, and why does a vector the grid only stretches grow at the rate of its stretch?","a":"with n free stretched vectors, every solution of x′ = Ax is c₁e^(λ₁t)v₁ + ⋯ + cₙe^(λₙt)vₙ, one list c for each start; along v the statement is x′ = λx, so x = e^(λt)v has slope λe^(λt)v = Ax","w":["every solution is e^(t)(c₁v₁ + ⋯ + cₙvₙ); every stretched vector grows at the rate 1","every solution is c₁λ₁ᵗv₁ + ⋯ + cₙλₙᵗvₙ; a stretched vector grows at the rate ln of its stretch","every solution is c₁e^(λ₁t) + ⋯ + cₙe^(λₙt) with the vectors dropped; the rate is the middle sum"],"why":"Along its stretched vectors the system splits into n one-unknown statements."},
 {"u":"27·cb24.2.1","q":"What is e^(tA), what does the grid-exponential technique say, and why was it needed when the split-system technique was in hand?","a":"the sum I + tA + t²A²/2! + ⋯, which settles for every square A and every t; x = e^(tA)x(0) is the one solution of x′ = Ax; it covers the grids with too few stretched vectors to split, as the slide, where it gives the t e^(rt) of a repeated root","w":["the grid with e^(t aᵢⱼ) in each place; it solves x′ = Ax only for grids that split","the sum I + tA + t²A² + ⋯, which settles for small t only; it was needed for grids with complex stretches","e^(t det A) times I; it was needed because the split-system technique gives no start"],"why":"e^(tA) is a sum of powers of the whole grid."},
 {"u":"27·cb24.3.1","q":"What are T and Δ of a 2 × 2 grid, and what does the sum-and-determinant technique say?","a":"T is the middle sum and Δ the determinant, the sum and the product of the two stretches; the paths of x′ = Ax form a saddle when Δ < 0, head in or out without turning when Δ > 0 and T² ≥ 4Δ, spiral when T² < 4Δ, in for T < 0 and out for T > 0, and close into loops when T = 0 < Δ","w":["T is the determinant and Δ the middle sum; the paths spiral when Δ < 0 and form a saddle when T < 0","T is the middle sum and Δ the determinant; every path heads in when T < 0, whatever Δ is","T is the largest entry and Δ the smallest; the paths close into loops when T = Δ"],"why":"Two numbers of the grid sort every path."},
 {"u":"27·cb24.4.1","q":"What is the tangent grid at a rest, what does the tangent-grid statement say, and when does the grid decide nothing?","a":"the grid of slopes of each rate in each unknown, taken at the rest; if every stretch has real part below 0 the rest holds, and if one has real part above 0 it does not; when a stretch sits on the line of real part 0 the grid cannot tell","w":["the grid A of the system itself; the rest holds when det is above 0; the grid decides nothing when it cannot be undone","the grid of slopes at the rest; the rest holds when every stretch is real; the grid decides nothing for complex stretches","the grid of the rates at the rest, all 0; the rest always holds; the grid decides everything"],"why":"A centre of the tangent grid may be a slow spiral in, a slow spiral out, or a true centre."},
 {"u":"27·cb24.5.1","q":"What are the two facts stated about a trapped path and about a pair of stretches crossing the line α = 0, and what does the loop-size technique say?","a":"in the plane a path held for ever in a closed, bounded region with no rest heads round a closed loop; when a pair α ± iβ crosses α = 0 as a knob passes a value, for most statements a small loop is born there and grows; and a near-circle path of y″ − (μ − y²)y′ + y = 0 neither gains nor loses energy over a turn exactly when its size is 2√μ","w":["a trapped path heads to a rest; a crossing pair makes the rest hold; the loop has size μ","a trapped path leaves its region in the end; a crossing pair makes a saddle; the loop has size 2μ²","a trapped path with no rest cycles; a crossing pair gives birth to a loop; the loop has size √μ/2, the same for every technique"],"why":"Smaller loops gain and grow; larger ones lose and shrink."},
 {"u":"27·cb24.6.1","q":"What is wrong with “T < 0, so every path heads in”, and with “the tangent grid has a centre, so the rest is a centre”?","a":"a saddle can have T < 0, so Δ > 0 is needed too; and a centre sits on the border line of real part 0, where the grid cannot tell: the left-over curve settles it","w":["T < 0 always gives a spiral in; and a centre of the grid is always a centre of the statements","T must be above 0 for paths to head in; and a centre of the grid means the rest does not hold","the first is right for 2 × 2 grids; the second fails only for saddles"],"why":"A pendulum with friction has a centre in a frictionless grid and still stops."});
/* end phase F und_r27 */
